// expect: 134
// A STATEFUL singleton op called BEFORE its handler's install has
// executed. The compile gate ADMITS this program by the belt-backed
// tier: a stateful-singleton demand with an install anywhere clears,
// because the row alone cannot split a dead extent from live dynamic
// coverage (the backtrack family's per-candidate installs are the
// legitimate face; the modal install-identity frontier, band A, owns
// the split). The SingletonUninstalled guard is the LOUD belt: at
// runtime early() finds the state global still 0 and refuses at the
// site (exit 134). On the pre-guard wheel this ran SILENTLY WRONG —
// the arm read its `n` field from the null page and wrote the state
// update back (the address-12 corruption class). The STATELESS twin's
// root-reaching escape refuses too, at its DISPATCH rather than at
// compile (tests/frontier/mn-effect-escaped-install.mn, re-banked
// 2026-09-11): the uniform world bracket leaves a dead extent with no
// world to run an arm under, so the walk refuses where the old
// stateless licence direct-called. A STATIC verdict there is band A's
// install identity — the row cannot see a closed extent, and the
// executable gate clears the effect because the handler is installed
// somewhere.

effect Counter {
  bump() -> Int
}

handler counting with n = 0 {
  bump() => resume(n + 1) with n = n + 1
}

fn early() with Counter = bump()

fn main() = {
  let a = early()   // drift-audit: ignore — the sequencing IS the fixture: this op call must execute BEFORE the install below
  let b = { bump() } ~> counting
  a + b
}
